            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Xerxes I</strong><br>\nXerxes I, born around 518 BCE, was the son of King Darius the Great and Atossa, daughter of Cyrus the Great. He was not the eldest of Darius's sons but was chosen as successor due to his lineage on both sides. Xerxes' upbringing was marked by luxury and education fit for a Persian prince, preparing him to take over the vast Persian Empire.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Ascension to the Throne</strong><br>\nXerxes I ascended to the throne in 486 BCE after the death of his father, Darius the Great. His early reign was spent consolidating his rule and suppressing rebellions. Egypt revolted against Persian rule, and Xerxes swiftly crushed the uprising, restoring Persian dominance over the region.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Persian Wars</strong><br>\nAs king, Xerxes I is best known for his role in the Persian Wars against Greece. He vowed to succeed where his father failed and planned a massive invasion to subjugate Greece. In 480 BCE, he led a large army and navy across the Hellespont on a campaign that would include the famous battles of Thermopylae, Salamis, and Plataea.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
