          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Battle of Thermopylae</strong><br>\nThe Battle of Thermopylae was a significant conflict during Xerxes' invasion. A small Greek force led by King Leonidas of Sparta held off the Persian army for three days. Although the Persians won the battle due to a traitor revealing a mountain path, the Greeks' heroic stand became legendary.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Naval Battle of Salamis</strong><br>\nFollowing Thermopylae, Xerxes faced the Greek fleet in the narrow straits of Salamis. Despite his naval superiority, the Persians were outmaneuvered and defeated by the smaller, more agile Greek ships. This defeat was a turning point in the Persian Wars, limiting Xerxes' advance into Greece.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Withdrawal to Asia</strong><br>\nAfter Salamis, Xerxes returned to Asia with a portion of his army, leaving his general Mardonius in charge of the remaining forces in Greece. The following year, Mardonius was killed, and his troops were decisively beaten at the Battle of Plataea, ending Persian ambitions in Greece.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
