          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Administration of the Empire</strong><br>\nXerxes focused on administrative affairs after his military campaigns. He continued his father's work, improving the empire's infrastructure and government. Xerxes was known for his construction projects in Persepolis, including the Hall of a Hundred Columns.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Cultural Achievements</strong><br>\nUnder Xerxes' rule, Persian art and culture flourished. He sponsored massive construction projects that expressed the empire's wealth and power. The splendor of his court and the luxuries he enjoyed became the subject of many ancient tales, showcasing the opulence of the Persian Empire at its zenith.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Assassination and Legacy</strong><br>\nXerxes I's reign ended with his assassination in 465 BCE. He was killed by a courtier in a conspiracy, and his son Artaxerxes I succeeded him. Xerxes' failed invasion of Greece overshadowed his achievements, but he also left a legacy as an administrator and builder which influenced Persian history for years to come.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
